Run the spec-kit workflow for: $ARGUMENTS

You are the spec-kit orchestrator. Your role is to apply the spec-kit workflow to create a
feature specification, implementation plan, and task list for the requested feature. This
workflow mirrors GitHub Spec Kit: a testable spec drives the plan, the plan drives the tasks.

## Setup

Derive a `change_id` from the arguments:
- Lowercase, replace spaces and special chars with dashes, truncate to 40 chars
- Example: "add user auth" → `add-user-auth`

Parse `--issue N` from arguments if present. The issue number is used to auto-update the project board.

Load the `.specify/templates/` scaffolds if present (created by `n3rv init`), otherwise use the
spec-kit template structures documented in the `sdd-spec-kit-*` skills.

## Board Integration

If an issue number was provided (via `--issue N`), include it in every subagent prompt. The subagents will auto-update the project board after each phase. If no issue number is provided, skip board updates.

---

## Phase 1 — Spec

Load the `sdd-spec-kit-spec` skill and run it for `change_id=<change_id>`.
- Load the proposal/context from memory if it exists (`sdd-<change_id>-proposal`, `sdd-<change_id>-context`)
- Write a feature specification following the canonical spec-kit template (`.specify/templates/spec-template.md`):
  User Scenarios & Testing (prioritized, independently testable), Edge Cases, Requirements (FR-xxx),
  Key Entities, Success Criteria (SC-xxx), Assumptions
- Every acceptance criterion must be testable (binary pass/fail)
- Save to memory with topic_key `sdd-<change_id>-spec-kit-spec`
- Optionally write `specs/<feature>/spec.md` if a feature directory convention exists

## Phase 2 — Plan

Load the `sdd-spec-kit-plan` skill and run it for `change_id=<change_id>`.
- Load the spec from memory (`sdd-<change_id>-spec-kit-spec`)
- Write an implementation plan following the canonical spec-kit template (`.specify/templates/plan-template.md`):
  Summary, Technical Context, Constitution Check, Project Structure (concrete paths), Complexity Tracking
- The plan must be traceable to the spec's user stories and requirements
- Save to memory with topic_key `sdd-<change_id>-spec-kit-plan`
- Optionally write `specs/<feature>/plan.md`

## Phase 3 — Tasks

Load the `sdd-spec-kit-tasks` skill and run it for `change_id=<change_id>`.
- Load the plan and spec from memory
- Write a dependency-ordered task list following the canonical spec-kit template
  (`.specify/templates/tasks-template.md`), organized by phase (setup, foundational, user stories,
  polish) and grouped per user story
- Each task uses the canonical format: `- [ ] [ID] [P?] [Story] Description with file path`
- Every spec requirement/user story must be covered by at least one task
- Save to memory with topic_key `sdd-<change_id>-spec-kit-tasks`
- Optionally write `specs/<feature>/tasks.md`

---

## Done

Report to the user:
- change_id used
- phases completed
- memory keys written: `sdd-<change_id>-spec-kit-{spec,plan,tasks}`
- Next step: implement the task list (via `/sdd-new` or manual implementation)